{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2689 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ad57db7edff8775ac11e9f16c7b1d59f4e4419b7ac54f4525c0c597745bd4e22" :edb/db-before-t 2688 :edb/db-after-t 2689 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536001 50 #inst "2026-10-15T17:23:54.343Z" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186054736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555830 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186054737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555849 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186054737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555863 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186054802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555859 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058267 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058268 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058269 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058270 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186058271 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555088 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555149 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555242 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555429 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555437 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186058266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555088 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059434 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186059434 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\nI. $(a-2, b-6)$\n\nII. $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\nIII. $(a+2, b-6)$\n\nIV. $(a-2,b+ 6)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059434 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2)^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059434 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186059633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555827 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555833 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555831 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555806 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186059878 1050 600.0 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060274 1071 "![](images/d5/d5da28420cf16c4563fbf7188a81b16c4cf8aaa62af39998456fe7016b76aa7d.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x)$, shown above?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186060274 1071 "![](images/d5/d5da28420cf16c4563fbf7188a81b16c4cf8aaa62af39998456fe7016b76aa7d.png)\n\nWhat is the domain of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060274 1074 "The function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Since there is a closed circle at $x=5,$ this point is included in the domain.\n\n- On the other hand, because there is an open circle at $x=-1,$ this point is not included in the domain.\n\nSo, the function $f(x)$ above is defined for values between $x=-1$ and $x=5,$ including $x=5$ but not including $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nx\\in (-1,5].\n$$\n\nNote that the function is defined at the point $x=3,$  even though the function jumps at that point. The function value at $x=3$ is $1.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060998 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\n \n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060998 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186060999 1064 "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186060999 1064 "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186061000 1064 "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n*If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n*If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186061000 1064 "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n> *If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n> *If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186068679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555823 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186068707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555776 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186068707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555798 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186068708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555785 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186073878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555775 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186073887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555869 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555846 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186083187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555853 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186083188 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555786 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186083310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555763 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186083891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082277 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186092331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555758 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186092331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555784 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186092331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555864 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186092332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555802 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186092332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555805 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095268 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095269 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095270 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095271 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095272 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555066 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555201 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555381 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555393 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555453 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186095267 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555066 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096588 1071 "What is the volume of a sphere whose diameter is $18 \\:\\textrm{cm}$?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186096588 1071 "What is the volume of a sphere whose diameter is $18 \\:\\textrm{cm}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096588 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{18}{2}=9\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\left(9\\right)^3 = 972\\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186096588 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3 ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\dfrac{18}{2}=9\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\left(9\\right)^3 \n \t\t= 972\\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096589 1071 "What is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3$.\n\n![](images/99/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186096589 1071 "What is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3.$\n\n![](images/99/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096589 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n36π &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n36π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} &= r^{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3\\:\\textrm{in}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186096589 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3 ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=36\\pi\\:\\textrm{in}^3$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 36\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt[3]{27} = 3\\:\\textrm{in}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555838 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555848 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555772 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555839 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555790 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555814 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555826 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186096914 1050 420.0 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186097223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186097223 1071 "![](images/26/26b1e5acba63b8ee11d35e12bf0c8fd6944b7a466c9eab258ef36f6429e8ec0a.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $320\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186097223 1071 "![](images/26/26b1e5acba63b8ee11d35e12bf0c8fd6944b7a466c9eab258ef36f6429e8ec0a.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $320πcm^{3}$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186097223 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=320\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 320\\pi \\cdot \\frac{3}{4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =240.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{240}=2\\sqrt[3]{30}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186097886 1064 "Given a sphere with radius\n\n$$\nr= 3\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nhow do we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/8d/8df0347cb30ac58b9cbcef4b7a7e25dfd35c899b1c833cdf2e8e22bcfc51971f.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV= \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=3\\:\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\cdot (3)^3 =36 \\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi$. However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation. In this case, we get\n\n$$\nV \\approx 113.10 \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186097886 1064 "Given a sphere with radius $r= 3\\:\\textrm{cm},$ how do we calculate its volume?\n\n![](images/8d/8df0347cb30ac58b9cbcef4b7a7e25dfd35c899b1c833cdf2e8e22bcfc51971f.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV= \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=3\\:\\textrm{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi \\cdot (3)^3\n=36 \\pi \\:\\textrm{cm}^3.\n$$\n\nWe often express the volume of a sphere as a multiple of $\\pi.$ However, we can also use a calculator to get a numerical approximation. In this case, we get\n\n$$\nV \\approx 113.10 \\:\\textrm{cm}^3\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186100807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555804 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186105706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555836 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186108968 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186108969 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186108970 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186108971 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186108972 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555265 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555308 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555347 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555405 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555495 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186108967 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555495 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109418 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109419 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109420 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109421 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109422 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555195 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555337 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555343 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555542 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555636 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109417 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555343 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109460 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109461 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109462 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109463 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109464 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555148 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555298 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555334 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555338 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555549 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109459 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555334 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109471 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109475 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109478 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109479 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109480 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109481 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186109482 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555311 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555332 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555397 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555410 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555621 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186109477 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555311 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555825 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555768 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555868 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555761 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555854 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555793 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555801 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555813 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555860 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110770 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555840 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555844 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186110783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555824 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111207 1071 "At the beginning of a study, a beehive had $4000$ bees. The study showed that the number of bees in the hive doubles every week. How many bees will be in the hive after $3$ weeks?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186111207 1071 "At the beginning of a study, a beehive had $4\\,000$ bees. The study showed that the number of bees in the hive doubles every week. How many bees will be in the hive after $3$ weeks?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111207 1074 "The hive starts with $4,000$ and then doubles every week. \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, there are $4\\,000\\cdot 2$ bees.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, there are $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{2}$ bees.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, there are $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{3}$ bees.\n\nSo the correct expression for the number of bees after $3$ weeks is $4\\,000\\cdot {2}^{3}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111282 1071 "![](images/0e/0e112d00ddcb34c30a490d1a71e3e0b4a2c698707e3890666ac4e299c3ca37cd.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠COA.$ If $m∠BOD={107}^{∘},$ find $m∠BOC.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111282 1071 "![](images/0e/0e112d00ddcb34c30a490d1a71e3e0b4a2c698707e3890666ac4e299c3ca37cd.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, and $OD^(→)$ is the bisector of $∠COA$. If $m∠BOD = 107^{∘}$, find $m∠BOC$." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186111282 1074 "![](images/2d/2d75ac364d71d8dd6cf35cfe7f8966b603c3cbee0bc5adff5f7dadf7468bab4c.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠DOA$ and $∠BOD$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠DOA & ={180}^{∘}-m∠BOD \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{107}^{∘} \\\\  & ={73}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm∠DOA=m∠COD={73}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =m∠BOD-m∠COD \\\\  & ={107}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\  & ={34}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111289 1071 "![](images/27/2772e28367d49fcf0e2503b7cdd5d5aae5ea217b7060b288f9fb6e149ee25f57.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the rectangle $LMNP$ above equals $22\\,cm.$ What is the length of $\\overset{―}{NP}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111289 1071 "![](images/27/2772e28367d49fcf0e2503b7cdd5d5aae5ea217b7060b288f9fb6e149ee25f57.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the rectangle $LMNP$ above equals $22cm$. What is the length of $NP^(―)$?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186111289 1074 "The opposite sides of a rectangle are congruent, so we have\n\n$$\nPL=MN=3\\,cm.\n$$\n\nFor the same reason, we also have\n\n$$\nNP=LM.\n$$\n\nNow, since the perimeter is $p=22\\,\\text{cm},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =LM+MN+NP+PL \\\\ 22 & =2NP+2PL \\\\ 22 & =2NP+6 \\\\ 16 & =2NP \\\\ 8 & =NP\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $NP=8\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111291 1074 "If $PQRS$ is a square, then $m∠QSP={45}^{∘}.$ Now,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QSP & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (2t+7)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 2t & =38 \\\\ t & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111292 1071 "![](images/5b/5b2ade26c577b7362460f55397024e3b321a54d769fe1c412f79f38b17f40806.png)\n\nIn a square above, $PO = 5mm$. Find $QS$." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186111292 1071 "![](images/5b/5b2ade26c577b7362460f55397024e3b321a54d769fe1c412f79f38b17f40806.png)\n\nIn a square above, $PO=5\\,mm.$ Find $QS.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186111292 1074 "The diagonals of a square are equal and bisect each other. So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QS & =PR \\\\  & =2PO \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5\\,mm \\\\  & =10\\,mm.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555795 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186119793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555769 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186119793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555856 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186119853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555807 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186121028 1064 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186121028 1064 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186121067 1064 "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186121067 1064 "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186122396 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122399 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186122402 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122404 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186122612 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122616 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124426 1071 "![](images/3c/3ccabfea2a80fc5f858400c5bac362841b8c6f6b41cdc6fedbbfcd031d2241ca.svg)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\nII. $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124426 1071 "![](images/85/859a831dcc11fa6ffe430cc913fa46283d3231816429d3fea98219fe3417b746.png)\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $\\mathbf{q}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector\n\n- $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{q}$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186124426 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](images/a1/a172ba62aed6c25fc1b40fdf70b1ae88a7e33d2f3341696783b96defd927b6be.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186124426 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that $P\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n![](images/e5/e51c398066c9be6bd62023005c888d115956938926a82f9d4695684f37421628.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a stochastic vector such that $P\\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{q}.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124489 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555789 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124489 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555847 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124490 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555820 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124490 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555871 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124592 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555837 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124592 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555861 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124615 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555828 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124712 1050 960.0 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124966 1071 "Consider the vector space $R_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${0, t, t^{2}}$ is a linearly independent set in $R_{2}[t]$\n2. $t^{2} - 2t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0, t, t^{2}}$\n3. ${0, t, t^{2}}$ is a basis of $R_{2}[t]$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186124966 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. ${t}^{2}-2t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124966 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2t=0\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot t+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124967 1071 "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${b_{1}, b_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n2. ${b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}, w}$ is linearly independent\n3. ${2b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ is a basis of $V$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186124967 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186124967 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125002 1071 "Consider the vector space $M_{2}(R)$ of all $2 \\cdot 2$ matrices over $R$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a linearly independent set in $M_{2}(R)$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. ${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is a basis of $M_{2}(R)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186125002 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125002 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125849 1064 "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186125849 1064 "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n![](images/ca/caba3c1b2f3245a3ebea0bd6ff6d47c63d409a7ba18a48db1f1269cb9ac13854.svg) \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n![](images/7f/7f231d09e7daf85eec90e682a9eb53c242aadac75b372e3685b65757ae3309dc.svg) \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125850 1064 "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186125850 1064 "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n![](images/77/77ffe4078d3c62c30ab94b59bc9465a92db659b9e70b137bb144deb44844e800.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n![](images/3d/3d91ece6103baa469569adf1994f3965cfc3429fbe65c0d7cfffff53b83b20ef.svg)\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/4a/4a5d81320f3e4eeee6b9fb09424238467d6c9e8b2e2246971ba3550b65a6d9c2.svg)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n![](images/85/8515cb27fa64a5186fae0c63184df2ff7651c03963eef5804c4d6bedbace2283.svg)" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125851 1064 "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n> *Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> converges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*\n\nIn other words, if [math]P[/math] is a regular [math]m \\times m[/math] stochastic matrix and [math]\\mathbf{q}_0 \\in \\mathbf{R}^m[/math] is an arbitrary (initial) stochastic vector, then [math]P[/math] has the unique steady state vector [math]\\mathbf{q}[/math] and \n\n\\[\nP^n \\mathbf{q}_0 \\to \\mathbf{q} \n\\]\n\nas [math]n \\to \\infty.[/math]" 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186125851 1064 "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n![](images/e9/e9588bb9c77762d6caee5460403077eba65a891676f85a9e30ff99e7e6112184.svg) \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n![](images/e0/e0ba33618820d19e77d365a9f316239ef2e36133cbcac4007bf2b5c2f6e57137.svg) \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n![](images/29/29c5a411ac3c797699a39e35972e415e98df3347e66581bb5494f61a92d92bd5.svg) \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n*Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nconverges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125855 1064 "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/44/44fa9ad32622bd1d7b3541206030ecc862a3db9420f46049c9f16992fdb947ca.svg) \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n![](images/03/030d5719a268d9e15c2c58488ff1c36602d5c757475e79c25dcd1da5a4eb8712.svg)\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186125855 1064 "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{bmatrix}\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126597 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126598 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126599 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126600 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126601 13194139536001 17592186045417 false] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555048 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555253 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555451 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555491 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186126596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555557 1319413953600