94139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555758 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555758 1071 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555758 1072 #uuid "0076dfd1-19a9-575a-953a-f75321364331" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555758 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5-2 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=7,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+7+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,14).\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555710 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1069 "q-335026" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1072 #uuid "021e3fe3-3d72-56fe-ac1a-be1f9dae2590" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555759 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555727 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1069 "q-277843" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1071 "The function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=32(2-x)^{3}-2.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1072 #uuid "03af7723-aeb4-55f1-8ed6-486c6b158e90" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555760 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y+4} & =k(2-x) \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y+4})}^{3} & ={(k(2-x))}^{3} \\\\ 2y+4 & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3} \\\\ 2y & ={k}^{3}(2-x)^{3}-4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}(2-x)^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{3}}{2}=32\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555734 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1069 "q-288569" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1072 #uuid "06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555761 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555660 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1069 "q-110462" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1072 #uuid "0dc73948-1028-504a-8a43-9f0c9272154c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555762 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of ${I}_{3}$ by $-\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555646 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1069 "q-275979" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1$ has a root in the interval $(-5,0),$ the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ equals{{field-1}}." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1072 #uuid "0e8ed785-b377-55fe-8149-0a31ad5b56e8" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555763 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $1$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }1}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-5,0)$ is $-1.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}+1$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =0-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=-1.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555700 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1069 "q-143774" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1072 #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555764 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555731 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1069 "q-90603" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈3.314\\,81,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(2x-5), & 3\\le x<5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1072 #uuid "15966dc5-b8d2-54f2-9092-584d157e0965" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555765 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{9}(2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ≈13.074\\,07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & ≈13.074\\,07-(3.314\\,81)^{2} \\\\  & ≈2.09\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555714 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1069 "q-148735" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1072 #uuid "16fdfda4-c2d1-55b9-ac4e-fb5a2493ec8f" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555766 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =-\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(-\\frac{3}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{27}{8}{x}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555644 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1069 "q-276777" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{2x-5}{x-4}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1072 #uuid "1a45b39d-afe3-5e4b-83e0-4037874d7882" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555767 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2x-5}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2y-5}{y-4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{2y-5}{y-4} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{2y-5}{y-4} \\\\ -x(y-4) & =2y-5 \\\\ -xy+4x & =2y-5 \\\\ 4x+5 & =xy+2y \\\\ 4x+5 & =y(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+5}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4x+5}{x+2}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555645 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1069 "q-86553" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1071 "Mike purchased an $80$'s baseball card for $$50.$ If the value of the card increases by $8%$ each year, what expression gives the card's value after $10$ years?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1072 #uuid "1b78dc69-6363-5c74-acb7-15e9e0faab04" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555768 1074 "The card's value is $$50$, and this number increases by $8%$ each year.\n\n$8%$ as a decimal is $0.08.$ Therefore, an increase of $8%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.08=1.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $10$ years, the value of the card is $50\\cdot (1.08)^{10}$ dollars." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555707 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1069 "q-106771" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1072 #uuid "1cf3361e-986e-5750-bf9f-73762dff3b0e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555769 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555639 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1069 "q-161603" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1072 #uuid "20069222-ef1d-5746-8188-77095ae40eed" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555770 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5d/5d8a94cd0df8095c78aec955c3d0f062e38410326399104644b24e981eb1db9a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555726 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1069 "q-140831" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\,∗\\, & \\frac{6}{7} & -\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{7} & \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1072 #uuid "20271074-591c-55b9-9da6-42faf357d0f6" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555771 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555709 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1069 "q-84976" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2(x-1)?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1072 #uuid "206046fc-72eb-5e7e-8330-c7dfc01fc134" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555772 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{2}{4})(x-1),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=1$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(1,0).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555750 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1069 "q-161628" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1072 #uuid "22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555773 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555756 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1069 "q-335263" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1072 #uuid "27a5f814-886e-5a61-acf9-facf209e99cf" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555774 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555672 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1069 "q-67769" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1071 "![](images/16/16e8f82922ebd5d4b10729e2935c893dd59a7dfb8faa69dde4567c7036209d7d.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $AC=BC$ and $\\overset{⟷}{CD}⊥\\overset{―}{AB}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1072 #uuid "29fce570-bbfd-5809-b558-27c71c49ba05" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555775 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the point $C$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, $\\overset{⟷}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $DA=DB.$ We can use this equation so solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DA & =DB \\\\ 3x-3 & =x+1 \\\\ 3x-x & =1+3 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555739 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1069 "q-66255" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1071 "Find the solutions of the equation $25{p}^{2}+9=0.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1072 #uuid "2ac537c1-52d1-57f6-aadf-0ba9eddf7efc" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555776 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{p}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ 25{p}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {p}^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\np=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{25}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $p$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{25}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555745 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1069 "q-143767" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{{X}^{4}+1},$ where $X\\in (-2,2)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{2}+X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1072 #uuid "2b66e4d9-8056-570c-838a-8b4a8b4a119c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555777 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+1}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-2,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{2}+x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\n.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555704 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1069 "q-143895" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y={X}^{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1072 #uuid "2ba326fb-1af3-5ddd-8698-9863f73e0845" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555778 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={X}^{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)={x}^{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ y & =({u}^{-1}(y))^{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =(2-2\\sqrt{y})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555675 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1069 "q-143779" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=-{X}^{2},$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,0)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1072 #uuid "2be8e1ac-e4a4-5fc5-ab2e-be1784629b6e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555779 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-{x}^{2}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,1).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{4}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555667 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1069 "q-42537" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1072 #uuid "2d00f9e4-bc18-5599-a1c2-2f30832c80ab" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555780 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of ${I}_{3}$ and subtracting the third row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{3}$ to $A.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555671 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1069 "q-148557" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1072 #uuid "300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555781 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-3} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y-3})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{3}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555751 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1069 "q-276631" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7-5x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1072 #uuid "306d067e-7a9d-57e7-9fd1-1469fa53a6b0" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555782 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{7-5x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{7-5y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{7-5y} \\\\ x(7-5y) & =2 \\\\ 7x-5xy & =2 \\\\ -5xy & =2-7x \\\\ 5xy & =7x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x-2}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{7x-2}{5x}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555677 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1069 "q-148432" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2x-6}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1072 #uuid "3136287b-ab52-50e3-8963-70ea96c4eb24" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555783 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2x-6}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2y-6}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2y-6} \\\\ x(2y-6) & =5 \\\\ 2xy-6x & =5 \\\\ 2xy & =6x+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{6x+5}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6x+5}{2x}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555702 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1069 "q-159750" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1071 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1072 #uuid "3261919b-dda9-5522-ac76-67204c4f5250" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555784 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}-4\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5+4 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=3,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot 3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =75+32-80 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-1,27).\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555706 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1069 "q-54628" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1071 "If $6{q}^{2}+2=0$, then $q=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1072 #uuid "33b0ba11-b389-5606-bdc5-3231dc03a907" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555785 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{q}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ 6{q}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ {q}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ {q}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\nq=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $q$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555705 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1069 "q-30392" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=k{x}^{2}+3x-15$ is $x=4.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1072 #uuid "35646e02-dadb-58eb-b337-8ae59fb6fd33" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555786 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $4.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=k$ and $b=3$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{2(k)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=4,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2k} & =4 \\\\ -3 & =8k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555742 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1069 "q-148588" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-kx}{x+1},\\,x\\ne -1\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{x+4},\\,x\\ne -4.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1072 #uuid "36115cd9-9382-506b-9404-8a68bbaccae0" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555787 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3-kx}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3-ky}{y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3-ky}{y+1} \\\\ x(y+1) & =3-ky \\\\ xy+x & =3-ky \\\\ xy+ky & =3-x \\\\ y(x+k) & =3-x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3-x}{x+k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{x+k}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555656 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1069 "q-148745" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1072 #uuid "374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555788 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ x-5 & =-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<5,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555754 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1069 "q-94869" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1072 #uuid "382e043a-e5b7-50fb-a8b9-9a5ba4e6f38b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555789 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555724 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1069 "q-118335" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1071 "What is the volume of a sphere whose diameter is $8\\,\\text{mm}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1072 #uuid "38d0237e-b5b0-553b-9c09-746f0f126714" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555790 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\,\\text{mm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {4}^{3}=\\frac{256}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555747 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1069 "q-110463" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1072 #uuid "3a8b7714-d243-5a18-865d-0e2cf918670b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555791 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the third row of ${I}_{3}$ and adding $7$ times the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1}$ to $A.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555703 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1069 "q-148539" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{kx-2}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3+2x}{5x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1072 #uuid "3bd71fc1-9e9b-5a64-b8c7-ee650f7e3c32" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555792 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{kx-2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{ky-2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{ky-2} \\\\ x(ky-2) & =3 \\\\ kxy-2x & =3 \\\\ kxy & =3+2x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3+2x}{kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3+2x}{kx}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=5.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555757 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1069 "q-223666" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x-1)(x-9)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1072 #uuid "3c9a4b7b-8e3b-5aa3-8825-873abec27dfc" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555793 1074 "From the equation $y=(x-1)(x-9),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=9.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=5.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555736 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1069 "q-148507" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x-8}{5x+1}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1072 #uuid "411f5065-9d51-51d9-906e-20eb90eb5d15" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555794 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{x-8}{5x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y-8}{5y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{y-8}{5y+1} \\\\ -x & =\\frac{y-8}{5y+1} \\\\ -x(5y+1) & =y-8 \\\\ -5xy-x & =y-8 \\\\ 8-x & =5xy+y \\\\ 8-x & =y(5x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{8-x}{5x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8-x}{5x+1}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555683 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1069 "q-106766" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1072 #uuid "42d466fd-6c03-513d-b916-3193ae5d935b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555795 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555664 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1069 "q-90605" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈1.136\\,90,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{14}, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1072 #uuid "42fc75dd-9bd8-5bf5-a346-8dddd7ab3b06" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555796 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{14}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{14}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{70}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1.578\\,57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & ≈1.578\\,57-(1.136\\,90)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555680 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1069 "q-110476" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation \n${R}_{3}:=\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1072 #uuid "44c4828d-5d0a-5c06-aad5-ea02f733cf44" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555797 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555753 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1069 "q-66253" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1071 "Find the solutions of the equation $16{y}^{2}+4=0.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1072 #uuid "45c755e7-98dd-5776-8bcf-226024a31c7b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555798 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking square root, we obtain:\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{4}}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $y$ using the imaginary unit $\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{\\sqrt{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555720 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1069 "q-146401" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=200$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,200.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{200}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1072 #uuid "490b7df2-1c00-5889-8265-81d6b29b18f3" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555799 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=60%=0.6,\\,n=200.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(200,0.6).\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555698 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1069 "q-143777" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\, & 1\\le x\\le e \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{X}{2},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1072 #uuid "4cdff0a1-3127-5db2-a31d-94794e2a46b7" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555800 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{X}{2}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{{u}^{-1}(y)}{2} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<e,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=e,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,u(e)=\\frac{e}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}, & \\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{e}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555688 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1069 "q-106157" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=-(x-5)(x+7).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1072 #uuid "4f8a844e-8d7a-52ec-932d-abb0df6525f2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555801 1074 "From the equation $y=-(x-5)(x+7),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-7.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+(-7)}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-1.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555699 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1069 "q-159804" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1071 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y\\le -2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1072 #uuid "4fcf6802-fc48-589d-8b96-17389df12736" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555802 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le -2).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le -2) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{-2-(-7)}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1) & =Φ(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le 1)=0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le -2)=0.8413.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555718 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1069 "q-148504" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{x+3}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1072 #uuid "500aed1e-922d-582e-a7e3-3bbfcff68302" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555803 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x-1}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2y-1}{y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2y-1}{y+3} \\\\ x(y+3) & =2y-1 \\\\ xy+3x & =2y-1 \\\\ 3x+1 & =2y-xy \\\\ 3x+1 & =y(2-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x+1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2-x}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555659 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1069 "q-110680" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1072 #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555804 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555676 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1069 "q-159922" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1071 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7673\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7704\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7734\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7995\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8023\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8238\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8289\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9564\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9573\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9641\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9649\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9656\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9713\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9719\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9726\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1072 #uuid "54165c1c-b41c-51d6-bf2e-f7ee2418543e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555805 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2+0+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+6+20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(0,40).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(-6<Y\\le 12).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-6<Y\\le 12) & =P(\\frac{-6-0}{\\sqrt{40}}<Z\\le \\frac{12-0}{\\sqrt{40}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90) & =P(Z\\le 1.90)-P(Z\\le -0.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.90)-Φ(-0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.95) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ(1.90) & =0.9713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.95)=1-Φ(0.95)=1-0.8289=0.1711.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.95<Z\\le 1.90)=0.9713-0.1711=0.8002.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-6<Y\\le 12)=0.8002.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555694 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1069 "q-121421" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1071 "The point  $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1072 #uuid "55975c7c-e140-5b83-8d6a-51f167ef8423" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555806 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-2,-1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+1,-1)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(-1))=(-1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,1+5)=(-1,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-2,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-2+1)=-1\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+5 \\\\  & =-(-1)+5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,6)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555678 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1069 "q-106755" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1072 #uuid "55b8ab85-3682-5003-bc82-d3e9f5248d03" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555807 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555721 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1069 "q-148734" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1072 #uuid "56a829fb-a840-50d6-85ba-f7cb746a9aa5" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555808 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =2(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(2(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =8(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=8(x-3)^{3}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555744 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1069 "q-148499" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x-4}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1072 #uuid "58d6a2d3-405c-5b5e-b02f-68804a6747aa" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555809 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3x+1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3y+1}{y-4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3y+1}{y-4} \\\\ x(y-4) & =3y+1 \\\\ xy-4x & =3y+1 \\\\ xy-3y & =4x+1 \\\\ y(x-3) & =4x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x+1}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4x+1}{x-3}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555658 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1069 "q-146398" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,80.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{80}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1072 #uuid "59f1c663-db33-5cbd-a122-ef9383054d3e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555810 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=72%=0.72,\\,n=80.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(80,0.72).\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555682 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1069 "q-161631" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1072 #uuid "5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555811 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}({(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555691 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1069 "q-110470" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1072 #uuid "5e29c468-7ba6-5997-83d8-49458f8bde8e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555812 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555674 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1069 "q-76803" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1071 "![](images/ff/ff7f82365a0b2253c43ad441a4af93354ae54ae027fbd2390634b64728cfb210.png)\n\nIn the figure shown above, determine the measure of $∠AOB.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1072 #uuid "5fcf3b89-deb2-57c7-9289-4881c9262fc3" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555813 1074 "From the diagram, $∠COF$ is a straight angle. So, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD+m∠DOE+m∠EOF & =m∠COF \\\\ {75}^{∘}+m∠DOE+{75}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠DOE & ={30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $∠AOB$ and $∠DOE$ are vertical angles. Therefore, since vertical angles are congruent, we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOB=m∠DOE={30}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555668 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1069 "q-13256" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1071 "![](images/cc/cc3274e21594169662d7b96d0fcadbab1b78a3d944445cc84aad4d37b395bed6.png)\n\nDetermine the volume of the sphere above." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1072 #uuid "65c6d3b5-044f-5981-b57b-636418a9ab94" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555814 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=1\\,\\text{in}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (1)^{3}=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555697 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1069 "q-270765" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1072 #uuid "672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555815 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555717 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1069 "q-143892" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\sqrt{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1072 #uuid "674cd7aa-b875-5b4c-b5f8-f279a4fd931b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555816 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\sqrt{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2y\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =2({y}^{2})\\cdot 2|y| \\\\  & =4{y}^{2}|y|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{y}^{3}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555655 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1069 "q-143757" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y={X}^{5}+1,$ where $X\\in (-1,1)$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2X,$ where $X\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nIII. $Y=1-{X}^{4},$ where $X\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1072 #uuid "68ad658e-fc68-5dd0-8313-20d02f0dbcab" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555817 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{5}+1$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Transformation III is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=1-{x}^{4}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555749 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1069 "q-148433" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6}{5x+3}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1072 #uuid "6ad34a02-d412-58b2-a634-d74158ac441a" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555818 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{6}{5y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{6}{5y+3} \\\\ x(5y+3) & =6 \\\\ 5xy+3x & =6 \\\\ 5xy & =6-3x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6-3x}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{6-3x}{5x}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555741 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1069 "q-161609" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1072 #uuid "6d03cab7-f35e-5f26-85d3-e871e46cdf45" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555819 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/14/140ac7f7d6036657a4ba8a0c6944474c2aa0dfba7f7719228cdffb4d0cf9316f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4={r}^{2}+4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555715 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1069 "q-36478" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1072 #uuid "7135217a-2b68-531f-be98-4ee4582b3546" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555820 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}+{b}_{3},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{2}+{b}_{3})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{3}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$, the given set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false since the vector ${b}_{3}$ can't be written as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}.$ But then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555642 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1069 "q-212479" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1071 "Consider the absolute value equation\n\n$$\n3|x+1|-4=8.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be expressed in the following equivalent form:\n\n$|x+1|=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1072 #uuid "72df5984-46fa-528d-8ad1-e8f21e9e20f3" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555821 1074 "We can rearrange the equation to isolate the absolute value on the left side:\n\nFirst, we add $4$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1|-4 & =8 \\\\ 3|x+1|-4+4 & =8+4 \\\\ 3|x+1| & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x+1| & =12 \\\\ \\frac{3|x+1|}{3} & =\\frac{12}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}|x+1|}{\\cancel{3}} & =4 \\\\ |x+1| & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555651 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1069 "q-148584" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2x+k}{x-1},\\,x\\ne 1\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-8}{x-2},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1072 #uuid "754f4c4b-11dc-5f96-9fa9-3c28fe7e8a39" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555822 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x+k}{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2y+k}{y-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2y+k}{y-1} \\\\ x(y-1) & =2y+k \\\\ xy-x & =2y+k \\\\ xy-2y & =x+k \\\\ y(x-2) & =x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{x+k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+k}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-8.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555693 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1069 "q-22902" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1071 "A right triangle has legs of length $2\\,\\text{cm}$ and $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1072 #uuid "763919e9-e5c7-5fb3-9ee3-feea81f9fd20" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555823 1074 "Let $a=2\\,cm,$ $b=4\\,cm,$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555661 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1069 "q-129313" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1071 "![](images/b6/b68ede517afc0d8fd6ce6a110ed261a805a3218d6afee3caaed88c73d6e3763e.png)\n\nFor which value of $t$ is the rectangle above also a square?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1072 #uuid "7693917f-3fc9-5b3a-9850-3e604834da65" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555824 1074 "If $PQRS$ is a square, then $m∠RSQ={45}^{∘}.$ Now,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠RSQ & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ (6t+3)^{∘} & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ 6t & =42 \\\\ t & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555643 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1069 "q-61834" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1071 "One month, a greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon. If the number of kilograms of sold watermelon doubles each month, how many kilograms of watermelon will the greengrocer sell after $4$ months?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1072 #uuid "7c172a4f-97f6-5647-a1db-039307497883" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555825 1074 "In a particular month, the greengrocer sold $40$ kilograms of watermelon, and then this amount doubles every month.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ month, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot 2$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $2$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{2}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $3$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{3}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\n- After $4$ months, the greengrocer will sell $40\\cdot {2}^{4}$ kilograms of watermelon.\n\nSo the correct expression is $40\\cdot {2}^{4}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555653 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1069 "q-33799" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1071 "![](images/bf/bf8bd7c7679e1c1df6c336b48892964c7658ac877b5efc22b099b1e98129d9ac.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1072 #uuid "8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555826 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343}{6}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{343}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555670 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1069 "q-46785" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ -2,\\, & x\\text{ }>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1072 #uuid "81bf18b7-eab3-5bbd-9ce6-359135969b2e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555827 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ we have $f(x)=1,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(0,1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(0,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=-2,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that begins at $(0,-2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](images/65/6527b4f7514f14211c796840c704df5544cabfa6099e69a3740e2e1941e126d2.png)" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555662 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1069 "q-135312" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.7 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.1 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n\nII. $Pq=q$\n\nIII. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1072 #uuid "81d21d06-3e96-5b9a-927f-4e61cf10e9c2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555828 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.7 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.1 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6.8}{38}+\\frac{7.7}{38}+\\frac{1.25}{19} \\\\ \\frac{3.4}{38}+\\frac{1.1}{38}+\\frac{3.25}{19} \\\\ \\frac{6.8}{38}+\\frac{2.2}{38}+\\frac{0.5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{17}{38} \\\\ \\frac{11}{38} \\\\ \\frac{5}{19}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555650 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1069 "q-143755" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=-3X,$ where $X\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ where $X\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $Y=\\sqrt{X},$ where $X\\in (0,\\infty )$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1072 #uuid "83ef6537-86b1-5578-a678-ff969ac2e505" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555829 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=-3x$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in [0,1].$\n\n- Transformation II is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is strictly decreasing for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555737 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1069 "q-160278" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1071 "What is the magnitude of the number $z=\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1072 #uuid "855d3f5e-247d-532b-b8b2-b14ad8b71bc3" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555830 1074 "The imaginary number $\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is equivalent to the complex number $z=0+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$\n\nWe know that for a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of $z=0+\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|z| & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555712 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1069 "q-158537" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={e}^{2x}-3?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1072 #uuid "8aef3688-a7db-5a18-95f7-9c000401998b" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555831 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={e}^{2x}-3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={e}^{2x}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{a}{2},b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y={e}^{2x}-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(\\frac{a}{2},b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{a}{2},b-3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={e}^{x}$, we have that ${e}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={e}^{2x}-3.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{a}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2\\cdot (a/2)}-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{a}-3 \\\\  & =b-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{a}{2},b-3)$ must lie on $y={e}^{2x}-3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555732 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1069 "q-276645" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}$ is defined for $x>-\\frac{7}{4}.$ Find ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1072 #uuid "8ea6de0b-579b-595d-b4da-dc4ce1abfbcc" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555832 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{(4x+7)^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{(4y+7)^{2}} \\\\ x(4y+7)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (4y+7)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y+7)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{x}} \\\\ |4y+7| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y+7 & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 4y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y>-\\frac{7}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n|4y+7|=4y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555748 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1069 "q-19294" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1071 "![](images/8d/8d531960f0aa1444b614279f1ab82d8cc01904580e44be331f42d3d28266e1a9.png)\n\nWhat is the definition of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1072 #uuid "8fda4d22-b124-5945-a801-91535484189e" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555833 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,4],$ we have a line with a slope of $1$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=x$ that ends at $(4,4),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(4,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(4,2).$ So the graph is the line $y=2$ that starts at $(4,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555695 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1069 "q-75162" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:=-8{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1072 #uuid "941b6a8e-c424-5e02-9c9b-4d19cc52c2d6" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555834 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operation is ${R}_{1}:=-8{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555686 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1069 "q-146405" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1072 #uuid "94f8387a-ce74-5bb3-92d4-c497c3ceea9c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555835 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=5%=0.05,\\,n=120.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(120,0.05)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =120\\cdot 0.05\\cdot (1-0.05) \\\\  & =5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555638 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1069 "q-212910" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1072 #uuid "97180f1c-2846-5a93-ae87-17ba0602d531" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555836 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot (\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{3\\cdot 7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 7}}{21} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555690 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1069 "q-107127" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2t+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{1,t,t+1\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1072 #uuid "9a623800-a9e7-562e-b4c1-2e4906af2084" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555837 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+(-1)\\cdot (t+1)=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere all three coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2t+1=0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot t+1\\cdot (t+1).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555752 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1069 "q-84968" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+5)^{2}=-3(y-6)?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1072 #uuid "9b1a0e36-dce4-509e-90b0-4f68fe84ad7d" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555838 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-5$ and $k=6.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-5,6).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555666 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1069 "q-115907" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=16x?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1072 #uuid "9b2b25e3-0202-54b4-aedb-ceb8981dd37f" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555839 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at the origin is\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4(4)x.\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=4.$ Therefore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555733 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1069 "q-26365" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1071 "![](images/65/65e89fcd8ae54d3c269c064f7c9f55a81f3a4dd847c4195550c8c2bf4251982f.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC$, respectively. If $m∠DOC=4x$ and $m∠COE=5x$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1072 #uuid "9de433c9-b3da-5c7d-a414-05618ea3bae5" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555840 1074 "![](images/77/77adc8fb9c00bd21579a8697f8b11c228e3864e657f2b991be4d09b4c6f5a96d.png)\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC$, we have \n\n$$\nm∠AOD=m∠DOC=4x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ is the bisector of $∠BOC$, we have \n\n$$\nm∠EOB=m∠COE=5x.\n$$\n\nNow, by the angle addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOD+m∠DOC+m∠EOB+m∠COE & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x+4x+5x+5x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 18x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555725 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1069 "q-161602" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=2$ and $z=4+y.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1072 #uuid "9ff4f4d5-934d-5904-8c7d-edf6b1d6934c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555841 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{2}^{4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555640 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1069 "q-148573" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5,\\,x>-k\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-2,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1072 #uuid "a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555842 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{2}{3(x-5)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\nk=2.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555723 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1069 "q-276634" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{6x+2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$  \n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1072 #uuid "a700c45d-e998-5eef-9aa2-096588d12ebe" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555843 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{6x+2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6y+2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{6y+2} \\\\ x(6y+2) & =5 \\\\ 6xy+2x & =5 \\\\ 6xy & =5-2x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5-2x}{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-2x}{6x}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555679 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1069 "q-129308" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1071 "![](images/c8/c897e2e9b209470f24964b5c00d4e79e4f056a2cbcab4e1e1a1651e949291537.png)\n\nIn a square above, $PO=2.5\\,m.$ Find $QS.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1072 #uuid "a8ecca8c-4ba9-51a9-9a23-0b141e2831c4" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555844 1074 "The diagonals of a square are equal and bisect each other. So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QS & =PR \\\\  & =2PO \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2.5\\,m \\\\  & =5\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555730 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1069 "q-146445" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1071 "Suppose that $38%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1072 #uuid "a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555845 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=38%=0.38,$ and $n=30.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =38%=0.38, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{30} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555728 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1069 "q-30451" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+12x+6.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1072 #uuid "aa0495c1-850f-5f82-bb9f-fd1f7fa6ce97" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555846 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ and $b=12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{12}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}+12x+6 \\\\  & =2(-3)^{2}+12(-3)+6 \\\\  & =18-36+6 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-3,-12).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555687 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1069 "q-107535" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1072 #uuid "ab0273b9-503a-530e-8fea-6b6048661bee" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555847 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555743 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1069 "q-115904" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x-4)^{2}=-24(y+5)?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1072 #uuid "b28f59d7-76e7-504d-aaab-0c27b75b3222" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555848 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(-6)(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-6.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=4$ and $k=-5.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(4,-5).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555746 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1069 "q-160292" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1071 "Find $|z|$ if $z=-k-2m\\text{i},$ where $k$ and $m$ are real numbers." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1072 #uuid "b2ac4656-c00e-5c47-8021-7036e619bd8c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555849 1074 "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=-k-2m\\text{i},$ we have $a=-k$ and $b=-2m.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|-k-2m\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+(-2m)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{k}^{2}+4{m}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555716 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1069 "q-100121" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1072 #uuid "b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555850 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2(-1)+2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(2(1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(1-1)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555663 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1069 "q-143775" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2-X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1072 #uuid "ba6a48c1-7aec-5ddc-a819-dca70319c91f" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555851 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2-X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2-x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2-y)\\cdot |-1| \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(2-y)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=2,\\,u(1)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4(2-y)^{3}, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555735 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1069 "q-143400" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1071 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is a sample of size $n=20$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =0.7$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate the value of $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1072 #uuid "c2a27711-6c10-58d5-b2f3-5f2c3c569a97" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555852 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1$ and the sample size $n=20,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{0.1}{20} \\\\  & =0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555722 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1069 "q-30452" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}-2x+1.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1072 #uuid "c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555853 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=-2$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{-2}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555649 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1069 "q-106134" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1071 "![](images/8c/8cd1c9ab91a23fae12dd45078ec44ca55e69da8aa787775d7a9583857a84cb71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{11}{6}$ and $\\frac{25}{6},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1072 #uuid "c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555854 1074 "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{25}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{25}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{36}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555729 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1069 "q-143773" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\, & 0<x<\\pi  \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=3X+3\\pi ,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1072 #uuid "c6f869b2-aa11-5377-8e76-cb36213ba7a6" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555855 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=3X+3\\pi .$ The transformation $u(x)=3x+3\\pi $ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =3y+3\\pi  \\\\ y & =3{u}^{-1}(y)+3\\pi  \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<\\pi ,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=3\\pi ,\\,u(\\pi )=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}-\\pi ), & 3\\pi <y<6\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555669 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1069 "q-32547" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1071 "Given that\n $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1072 #uuid "d20ab7a7-2299-5eca-8c7e-9709ddaf034c" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555856 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555673 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1069 "q-131169" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1072 #uuid "d36205bb-47f5-5c37-9085-d080474849b2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555857 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}+\\frac{30}{5{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{3{x}^{3}}+\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555647 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1069 "q-143764" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1072 #uuid "d414a04e-d0c3-5f7e-b95a-0d762c3601e3" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555858 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{2}X-2.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}y-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1}(y)-2 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(2y+4)\\cdot |2| \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2y+4)^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $2<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=2$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(2)=-1,\\,u(4)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{(y+2)^{2}}, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555692 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1069 "q-54292" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1071 "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1072 #uuid "d96a4476-f395-552e-94ef-9cd8de4e9f95" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555859 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555665 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1069 "q-117152" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1071 "![](images/18/187b52aa0450e940bd644f9d1ae560a735c7e10e18157ecba910a6a817c11797.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle,  and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={58}^{∘},$ find $m∠COA.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1072 #uuid "da69f84c-0e57-51e1-a173-b362df890bdd" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555860 1074 "![](images/9d/9d7c5789c579b9292beb17ad03ae6239083ffa70fe5ec581da33cd488e365344.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={122}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{122}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={61}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={61}^{∘}+{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={119}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555654 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1069 "q-107132" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nII. $3t-2{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1072 #uuid "da9b8a15-279d-5af5-bedd-335abf7ae54a" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555861 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3t-2{t}^{3}=3\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $1+t$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set\n$\\{t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555681 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1069 "q-148508" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5-2x}{x+1}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1072 #uuid "de7ae964-9fce-58b0-bd1b-ba5303123396" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555862 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5-2x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5-2y}{y+1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5-2y}{y+1} \\\\ x(y+1) & =5-2y \\\\ xy+x & =5-2y \\\\ xy+2y & =5-x \\\\ y(x+2) & =5-x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5-x}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5-x}{x+2}.\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555738 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1069 "q-16851" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1071 "Find $|z|$ if $z=2c-5d\\text{i},$ where $c$ and $d$ are real numbers." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1072 #uuid "df8c7ab8-3861-5c49-964d-3d49c4f6daab" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555863 1074 "For a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=2c-5d\\text{i},$ we have $a=2c$ and $b=-5d.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|2c-5d\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(2c)^{2}+(-5d)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{c}^{2}+25{d}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555701 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1069 "q-159749" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1071 "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1072 #uuid "e1d8b86d-ac0b-5d14-99b3-68fba3ea8058" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555864 1074 "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=3,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+12-24 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(11,33).\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555740 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1069 "q-276781" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{3k}{6+4x}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9-6x}{4x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1072 #uuid "e33bcefe-71b8-5e01-9202-e30bde07e783" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555865 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3k}{6+4x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3k}{6+4y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3k}{6+4y} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3k}{6+4y} \\\\ x(6+4y) & =3k \\\\ 6x+4xy & =3k \\\\ 4xy & =3k-6x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k-6x}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3k-6x}{4x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555708 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1069 "q-143756" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1071 "Suppose that $X$ is a continuous random variable. For which of the following transformations can the change-of-variables method be applied to find the probability density function of $Y?$\n\nI. $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ where $X\\in [1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $Y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X,$ where $X\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$\n\nIII. $Y={X}^{3},$ where $X\\in (-10,10)$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1072 #uuid "e6df473a-21d5-53f9-84c7-d9c14b1c57fc" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555866 1074 "The change-of-variables method can be applied when a transformation $Y=u(X)$ is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing (i.e., strictly monotonic) over the entire support of $X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Transformation II is *not* strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)$ and strictly decreasing for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$\n\n- Transformation III is strictly monotonic. The function $u(x)={x}^{3}$ is strictly increasing for $x\\in (-10,10).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555648 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555689 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1069 "q-335432" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1072 #uuid "ea042103-925d-51d6-8c12-93baa97ad8a2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555867 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\, \\\\ \\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555641 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1069 "q-61650" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1071 "Rose's initial salary was $$10\\,000$, and her salary increased by $12%$ per year. To the nearest $$10$, what was Rose's salary $3$ years later?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1072 #uuid "ebeb4e55-43ec-53a6-b8e8-b7cfc99f6520" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555868 1074 "Rose received $$10\\,000$ initially, and this amount increased by $12%$ each year.\n\n$12%$ as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, an increase of $12%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.12=1.12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Rose received $10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money received after $3$ years, we get\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot (1.12)^{3}≈14\\,050.\n$$\n\nAfter $3$ years, Rose received approximately $14\\,050$ dollars." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555657 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1069 "q-60049" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1071 "Isabella took $2$ hours to wash $12$ windows. If she washes the windows at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1072 #uuid "ede2a25e-f545-5338-983c-ea1ddced5b57" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555869 1074 "The ratio of washed windows to hours is $12:2.$ So we start with the ratio $12:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Isabella washed $6$ windows per hour." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555685 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1069 "q-94051" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1072 #uuid "f4374452-85e0-5d4e-85af-02cf21a24ee9" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555870 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555684 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1069 "q-107938" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1072 #uuid "f4424f4e-4e2c-59a3-8d15-5c180b952f0f" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555871 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent. Hence, the given set is not a basis for $V.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}({b}_{1}+{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555755 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1069 "q-148537" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{7x+k}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1+3x}{7x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1072 #uuid "fa405f43-aa49-5df2-9659-18d1b520e384" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555872 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{7x+k}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{7y+k}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{7y+k} \\\\ x(7y+k) & =1 \\\\ 7xy+kx & =1 \\\\ 7xy & =1-kx \\\\ y & =\\frac{1-kx}{7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1-kx}{7x}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555652 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1069 "q-143894" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X},$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1072 #uuid "ff19e38b-ccbe-5836-a668-3a89307b9227" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555873 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({y}^{3})\\cdot |3{y}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{({y}^{3})}^{3}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{9}}{4}\\cdot 3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<2,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=0,\\,u(2)=\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{11}}{4}, & 0<y<\\sqrt[3]{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555874 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555925 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555874 1062 #uuid "00d8fa95-6258-55b6-83d5-e04b9f3228b7" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555874 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555852 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555875 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555941 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555875 1062 #uuid "01c244ab-6c29-59ab-8921-3e9ab03051b2" 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555875 1063 #edb/ref 17592186555864 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186555876 1058 #edb/ref 17592186555956 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [1759218